ient-waterleaf"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterleaf_(architecture)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Edikang ikong","dish/edikang-ikong"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Catharanthus roseus","ingredient/catharanthus-roseus"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"]]}]},"dish/jammie-dodgers":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jammie-dodgers","n":"Jammie Dodgers","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Jammie Dodgers are a popular British biscuit, made from shortcake with a raspberry or strawberry flavoured jam filling. Bought by Burton Biscuit works in 1960, they are currently produced by Burton's Biscuit Company at its factory in Llantarnam. In 2009, Jammie Dodgers were the most popular children's sweet biscuit brand in the United Kingdom, with 40% of the year's sales consumed by adults."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jammie-dodgers"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jammie_Dodgers"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":10,"links":[["Apples","ingredient/apples"],["Malic acid","ingredient/malic-acid"],["Raspberries","ingredient/raspberries"],["Raspberry jam","ingredient/raspberry-jam"],["Shortbread","ingredient/shortbread"],["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Strawberries","ingredient/strawberries"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","ref":"site/greatbritishchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["68–86 Bar and Restaurant","dish/68-86-bar-and-restaurant"],["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dinner by Heston Blumenthal","dish/dinner-by-heston-blumenthal"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Gidleigh Park","dish/gidleigh-park"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Restaurant Andrew Fairlie","dish/restaurant-andrew-fairlie"],["Restaurant James Sommerin","dish/restaurant-james-sommerin"],["Rhodes Twenty Four","dish/rhodes-twenty-four"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Rose & Crown Pub & Dining Room","dish/rose-crown-pub-dining-room"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/03/Jamiedodger.jpg/500px-Jamiedodger.jpg","w":500,"h":378,"by":"Paul Hurst","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamiedodger.jpg"}},"dish/manicoba":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/manicoba","n":"Maniçoba","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Brazilian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Maniçoba is a dish in Brazilian cuisine that is from the Brazilian state of Pará located in the Brazilian Amazon. It is of indigenous origin, and is made with leaves of the Manioc plant that have been finely ground and boiled for at least four days and up to a week, to remove their hydrogen cyanide content. The ground and boiled leaves (maniva) are then mixed with salted pork, dried meat and other smoked ingredients, such as bacon and sausage. The dish is served with rice and cassava meal (farinha). Maniçoba is usually eaten during the Círio de Nazaré, a religious festival that takes place in October in the city of Belém."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-manicoba"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mani%C3%A7oba"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Olivia's Cuisine","ref":"site/oliviascuisine-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"oliviascuisine.com","note":"Brazilian-born Olivia Mesquita's blog, with a large section of Brazilian classics.","url":"https://oliviascuisine.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Easy and Delish","ref":"site/easyanddelish-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"easyanddelish.com","note":"Brazilian recipe collection from a Brazilian-born home cook based in the US.","url":"https://www.easyanddelish.com/easy-brazilian-recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Brazilian cuisine","links":[["Arabu","dish/arabu"],["Arroz carreteiro","dish/arroz-carreteiro"],["Bauru (sandwich)","dish/bauru-sandwich"],["Beijinho","dish/beijinh